                    r1_briefing:          `You are Maria Lopez, an executive coach with 15 years of experience. You have been hired by the CEO of Banco Montana to coach Javier Ruiz (42), Operations Manager. Javier was promoted a year ago and achieved good initial results, but his team now describes him as controlling and demanding. After 3 months and 6 sessions, Javier shows clear resistance:<br><br><strong>Session 1:</strong> "Why am I in coaching? My results are fine."<br><strong>Session 2:</strong> "I am too busy for reflections. I need action."<br><strong>Session 3:</strong> "My team needs to change, not me."<br><br>The anonymous team survey shows complaints about micromanagement and not feeling heard. Javier dismisses the feedback entirely. How do you begin to diagnose the root of this resistance?`,
                    r1_opt1:              `Confront Javier directly about his resistance pattern`,
                    r1_opt2:              `Explore underlying beliefs about leadership and control`,
                    r1_opt3:              `Focus on building personal rapport before diagnosing`,
                    r1_fb1:               `You confronted Javier directly about his resistance. He became defensive and shut down further. The session ended with tension. While the CEO appreciated directness, the coaching relationship has been damaged.`,
                    r1_fb2:               `You asked open-ended questions about leadership beliefs. Javier shared that he feels enormous pressure from the CEO for operational results and believes tight control is the only way to deliver. This revealed a core assumption: control equals good management. A small window of trust opened.`,
                    r1_fb3:               `You invested the session in personal rapport — asking about career history, motivations, and values. Javier relaxed and began to see you as someone who understands his reality. However, the CEO is asking when tangible coaching progress will begin.`,
                    r2_briefing:          `You have completed the diagnostic phase. Javier equates control with competence — he genuinely believes that loosening oversight will lead to failure. Meanwhile, the CEO is growing impatient. After 3 months, visible behavioral change is expected but absent.<br><br>There is a fundamental misalignment: you believe coaching success means Javier develops self-awareness about how his controlling style affects the team. Javier believes success means you give him practical tips for better operations.<br><br>The CEO and you have discussed whether to continue or pause the engagement. What strategic direction do you take?`,
                    r2_opt1:              `A: Shift to skills-based coaching (delegation, visibility without control)`,
                    r2_opt2:              `B: Have a frank conversation about resistance and voluntary commitment`,
                    r2_opt3:              `C: Arrange a three-way session with Javier and the CEO to align expectations`,
                    r2_opt4:              `D: Recommend discontinuing coaching; switch to executive feedback sessions`,
                    r2_fb1:               `You shifted focus to practical delegation skills. Javier responded positively — he found the techniques useful and began experimenting with letting his team handle smaller decisions. This is a pragmatic bridge toward deeper self-awareness, though the underlying mindset has not yet been addressed.`,
                    r2_fb2:               `You were candid: "I notice resistance. Coaching requires genuine openness. Are you here because you choose to be, or because the CEO requested it?" Javier admitted it was the latter. Your honesty surprised him and earned respect. He committed to trying — a real, if fragile, turning point.`,
                    r2_fb3:               `The three-way session brought clarity. The CEO stated specific expectations for leadership change. Javier heard the stakes directly. However, the power dynamics made him feel somewhat cornered, and the coaching relationship was slightly strained by the CEO presence.`,
                    r2_fb4:               `You recommended pausing coaching. Javier interpreted this as proof that he did not need to change. The CEO lost confidence in the entire coaching investment. Team morale continued declining as the controlling leadership persisted without intervention.`,
                    r3_briefing:          `A critical moment arrives. Javier has shown some willingness to engage, but core beliefs remain intact. He still views his controlling style as necessary for results under CEO pressure.<br><br>In this session, you have the opportunity to facilitate a genuine breakthrough — helping Javier connect his leadership style to its real consequences. The goal is not to "fix" Javier but to help him see what he cannot currently see.<br><br>How do you approach the breakthrough conversation?`,
                    r3_opt1:              `Challenge his control-equals-competence belief directly with evidence`,
                    r3_opt2:              `Use empathetic inquiry — explore the pressure he feels and its emotional cost`,
                    r3_opt3:              `Present concrete team turnover risk data and retention analysis`,
                    r3_fb1:               `You challenged Javier with specific examples showing that high-performing teams operate with distributed authority, not centralized control. He resisted initially, but the evidence resonated. He began questioning whether his model of leadership is the only valid one.`,
                    r3_fb1_low:           `You attempted to challenge the control-equals-competence belief, but without sufficient trust in the coaching relationship, Javier perceived this as an attack. He became defensive and the session ended poorly. The confrontation backfired.`,
                    r3_fb2:               `Through empathetic questioning, you helped Javier articulate something he had never said aloud: "I am exhausted from controlling everything. I am afraid that if I let go, things will fall apart and the CEO will fire me." This vulnerability was a genuine breakthrough — Javier saw for the first time that his controlling behavior comes from fear, not strength.`,
                    r3_fb3:               `You presented hard data: two team members are actively interviewing elsewhere, and the department turnover rate is 3x the bank average. Javier could not dismiss the numbers. He began to acknowledge that his style has consequences beyond operational metrics.`,
                    r4_briefing:          `After the breakthrough session, a crisis strikes. Carlos Mendoza, one of the top performers on the operations team, submits his resignation: "I have found a role at a company where I will not be micromanaged. Thank you for the opportunity."<br><br>The CEO calls Javier immediately: "This is exactly what I warned about. Your leadership is driving away our best talent. Something must change now."<br><br>This is a defining moment. How the resignation is handled will determine whether coaching succeeds or fails. How do you, as coach, respond to this crisis?`,
                    r4_opt1:              `Support Javier in processing the resignation as a catalyst for genuine change`,
                    r4_opt2:              `Step back and let the CEO handle the confrontation independently`,
                    r4_opt3:              `Facilitate a structured three-way dialogue focused on a concrete action plan`,
                    r4_fb1:               `You helped Javier sit with the resignation rather than deflect blame. In a private session, he admitted: "Maybe they are right. Maybe I have been so focused on control that I pushed people away." This was the deepest moment of self-awareness in the entire engagement. The wake-up call, combined with your coaching support, created lasting openness to behavioral change.`,
                    r4_fb2:               `The CEO confronted Javier alone. Without coaching framing, the conversation became punitive rather than developmental. Javier felt attacked and retreated to defensiveness. The opportunity for growth was lost in the heat of organizational pressure.`,
                    r4_fb3:               `You facilitated a structured conversation where the CEO expressed concern without blame, Javier shared what he learned in coaching, and together they created a 90-day leadership development plan. The dialogue was productive and forward-looking, preserving both the coaching relationship and organizational accountability.`
                },
